  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 2,
    "lesson_title": "Deriving the Output for a Complex Exponential Input",
    "lesson_core_move": "Derive the LTI output for $x(t)=e^{j\\omega t}$ by substituting the input into the convolution integral and factoring out $e^{j\\omega t}$ to identify the remaining integral as the system's complex gain at $\\omega$.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Deriving the Output for a Complex Exponential Input",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Deriving the Output for a Complex Exponential Input",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Treat $x(t)=e^{j\\omega t}$ as a probe input for an LTI system. The goal of this step is to set up the convolution so the factor that depends only on $t$ will be easy to see in the next algebraic move.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(t-\\tau)h(\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Write the convolution in shifted-input form so the substitution matches $x(t-\\tau)$ exactly. With that form in place, insert the input as $x(t-\\tau)=e^{j\\omega(t-\\tau)}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t-\\tau)=e^{j\\omega(t-\\tau)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now the output is written with the complex exponential inside the integral, and the integrand is ready to be separated into its $t$-dependent and $\\tau$-dependent parts. That separation is the move that will expose the system's complex gain at $\\omega$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}e^{j\\omega(t-\\tau)}h(\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Keep this setup in mind: the next step will rewrite the exponential, factor out $e^{j\\omega t}$, and leave the remaining $\\tau$-integral as the frequency-response factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Deriving the Output for a Complex Exponential Input",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Substituting $e^{j\\omega t}$ into the LTI Convolution Integral",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Start from $y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(t-\\tau)h(\\tau)\\,d\\tau$ and substitute $x(t-\\tau)=e^{j\\omega(t-\\tau)}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the shifted-input convolution form. The cue is the slot $x(t-\\tau)$ inside $y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(t-\\tau)h(\\tau)\\,d\\tau$; in this step, replace only that input term and leave $h(\\tau)$ unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(t-\\tau)h(\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute the probe input directly into the shifted slot: $x(t-\\tau)=e^{j\\omega(t-\\tau)}$. Keep the $t-\\tau$ dependence intact.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}e^{j\\omega(t-\\tau)}h(\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That is the finished example result. Stop before factoring out $e^{j\\omega t}$; this step is only about making the substitution cleanly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Deriving the Output for a Complex Exponential Input",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Rewriting $e^{j\\omega(t-\\tau)}$ to Separate $t$ and $\\tau$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Rewrite $e^{j\\omega(t-\\tau)}$ so the factor depending only on $t$ is separated from the factor depending only on $\\tau$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the shifted exponential inside the integral. Use the exponent rule to split the factor that depends only on $t$ from the factor that depends only on $\\tau$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}e^{j\\omega(t-\\tau)}h(\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the difference rule for exponents: $e^{a-b}=e^ae^{-b}$. Here that gives $e^{j\\omega(t-\\tau)}=e^{j\\omega t}e^{-j\\omega\\tau}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}e^{j\\omega t}e^{-j\\omega\\tau}h(\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The result is the same integral with the $t$ dependence now isolated as $e^{j\\omega t}$. Do not factor it out yet; this step only makes the separation visible.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Deriving the Output for a Complex Exponential Input",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Factoring $e^{j\\omega t}$ Outside the Integral",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Take the separated form of the integrand and pull $e^{j\\omega t}$ outside the integral.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that $e^{j\\omega t}$ does not depend on $\\tau$. When a factor is independent of the integration variable, move it outside and leave the $\\tau$-integral unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}e^{j\\omega t}e^{-j\\omega\\tau}h(\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now factor out the $t$-only term and keep the remaining $\\tau$-dependent part inside the integral.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=e^{j\\omega t}\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}e^{-j\\omega\\tau}h(\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the factored form the later step will interpret. The output now has a pure $t$ factor multiplying a single constant integral.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Deriving the Output for a Complex Exponential Input",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Identifying the Remaining Integral as the Complex Gain at $\\omega$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Recognize the remaining $\\tau$-integral as the system's complex gain at $\\omega$ and rewrite the output in gain-times-input form.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the remaining $\\tau$-integral after the factorization step. Name that integral $H(j\\omega)$; it is the system's complex gain at the probe frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=e^{j\\omega t}\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}e^{-j\\omega\\tau}h(\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Define the remaining integral as $H(j\\omega)$ so the frequency-dependent system response has a clear name.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(j\\omega)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}e^{-j\\omega\\tau}h(\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substituting that definition gives the final gain-times-input form. The same complex exponential stays at frequency $\\omega$, while the system contributes the complex multiplier $H(j\\omega)$, which changes amplitude and phase.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=H(j\\omega)e^{j\\omega t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
